70445162873e-07,-0.0008989107445813715,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-0.014784417115151882,-0.44793957471847534,-7.152555099310121e-07,-0.0632614716887474,-8.21318244561553e-05,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,0.0,0.0,-0.0012677022023126483,-0.18868693709373474,-2.861018856492592e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,785,738,315,3501,4930,87,7358,22669,15087,429,26553,198,78045,17,87,284,220,18,88,284,481,89,59,60,285,264,1555,382,785,738,315,3501,4930,87,7358,22669,15087,429,26553,198,78045,21,87,284,481,88,284,481,19,89,59,60,285,2441,1555,382,9885,279,9210,1948,1493,5128,11,304,12348,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe set of points $(x,y,z)$ that satisfy\n\\[2x = 3y = -z\\]is a line.\n\nThe set of points $(x,y,z)$ that satisfy\n\\[6x = -y = -4z\\]is another line.\n\nFind the angle between these lines, in degrees.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"f045b09de53842fd533792ede3e908086bf157ae91bc9b45e66d907607a07bd7"},"seconds":38.22110986709595,"seed":41562576851462818,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the angle between two lines defined by the given equations, we need to first find the direction vectors of each line. The angle between the lines is the same as the angle between their direction vectors.\n\nThe first line is defined by the equation\n\\[2x = 3y = -z.\\]\nWe can parameterize this line by setting \\(2x = 3y = -z = t\\), where \\(t\\) is a parameter. Then we have:\n\\[x = \\frac{t}{2}, \\quad y = \\frac{t}{3}, \\quad z = -t.\\]\nThus, the direction vector for the first line is\n\\[\\mathbf{v}_1 = \\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{3}, -1 \\right\\rangle.\\]\n\nThe second line is defined by the equation\n\\[6x = -y = -4z.\\]\nWe can parameterize this line by setting \\(6x = -y = -4z = s\\), where \\(s\\) is a parameter. Then we have:\n\\[x = \\frac{s}{6}, \\quad y = -s, \\quad z = -\\frac{s}{4}.\\]\nThus, the direction vector for the second line is\n\\[\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\left\\langle \\frac{1}{6}, -1, -\\frac{1}{4} \\right\\rangle.\\]\n\nTo find the angle \\(\\theta\\) between the two lines, we use the dot product formula:\n\\[\\mathbf{v}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\|\\mathbf{v}_1\\| \\|\\mathbf{v}_2\\| \\cos \\theta.\\]\nFirst, we compute the dot product \\(\\mathbf{v}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2\\):\n\\[\\mathbf{v}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{3}, -1 \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\left\\langle \\frac{1}{6}, -1, -\\frac{1}{4} \\right\\rangle = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (-1) + (-1) \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right) = \\frac{1}{12} - \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{12} - \\frac{4}{12} + \\frac{3}{12} = 0.\\]\nSince the dot product is 0, the vectors are orthogonal, meaning the angle \\(\\theta\\) between the lines is \\(90^\\circ\\).\n\nTherefore, the angle between the lines is \\(\\boxed{90}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{90}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
